Subject: [PATCH 2/2] libvncclient: handle QEMU key ACK before pixel bounds
Author: Omaware contributors
Date: 2026-09-27
Upstream-Status: Not submitted; local compatibility fix
License: GPL-2.0-or-later (same as modified upstream file)
Base: LibVNCServer-0.9.15 9b54b1ec32731bd23158ca014dc18014db4194c3
Depends-On: 041ea576c3dddd6c7169935aaf8889673024fbfc

QEMU's set_encodings() sends its ExtendedKeyEvent capability ACK before
vnc_desktop_resize(). The ACK contains current server surface dimensions,
which may exceed the dimensions sent in ServerInit. It has no pixel data.
Handle it with the other pseudo-rectangles before validating pixel bounds.
Keep ordinary framebuffer rectangle validation unchanged.

The associated socketpair regression fails with both clean 0.9.15 and the
screen-ID-zero backport alone, and passes with this additional fix. This is
an original local patch, not a backport of an upstream commit.

--- a/src/libvncclient/rfbclient.c
+++ b/src/libvncclient/rfbclient.c
@@ -2127,6 +2127,13 @@
 	continue;
       }
       
+      /* A capability ACK has no pixels. QEMU can send its new surface size
+       * here before sending the corresponding framebuffer resize. */
+      if (rect.encoding == rfbEncodingQemuExtendedKeyEvent) {
+        SetClient2Server(client, rfbQemuEvent);
+        continue;
+      }
+
       if (rect.encoding == rfbEncodingKeyboardLedState) {
           /* OK! We have received a keyboard state message!!! */
           client->KeyboardLedStateEnabled = 1;
@@ -2533,10 +2540,6 @@
      }
 
 #endif
-
-      case rfbEncodingQemuExtendedKeyEvent:
-        SetClient2Server(client, rfbQemuEvent);
-        break;
 
       default:
 	 {
